<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paramahansa Yogananda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mukunda Lal Ghosh, Swami Yogananda Giri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paramahansa Yogananda, born Mukunda Lal Ghosh in Gorakhpur, was an Indian and American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monk, yogi and guru who founded the Self-Realization Fellowship and the Yogoda Satsanga Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to spread the teachings of Kriya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoga">Yoga</a>. A disciple of Swami Sri Yukteswar Giri, he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1920, toured the country as a speaker and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> in 1925. In 1927 he was received at the White House by President Calvin Coolidge. His Autobiography of a Yogi, published in 1946, has sold over four million copies, and his influence on the American yoga movement led to his being called the Father of Yoga in the West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paramahansa-yogananda.jpg" alt="Paramahansa Yogananda" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paramahansa_Yogananda_Standard_Pose.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yogananda was born Mukunda Lal Ghosh on 5 January 1893 into a Bengali Kayastha family, the fourth of eight children of Bhagabati Charan Ghosh, vice president of the Bengal-Nagpur Railway, and Gyanprabha Devi. His father's work took the family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bareilly">Bareilly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>.</p><p>From 1910 to 1920 he trained under Sri Yukteswar at hermitages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> and Puri, and graduated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore-college">Serampore College</a>. In July 1915 he took vows into the monastic Swami order and chose the name Swami Yogananda Giri. In 1917 he founded a school for boys at Dihika, later moved to Ranchi, which combined modern education with yoga training and became the Yogoda Satsanga Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1920 he accepted an invitation from the American Unitarian Association to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s delegate to an International Congress of Religious Liberals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. In his Autobiography of a Yogi he writes that Mahavatar Babaji, the foremost guru of his lineage, appeared to him and told him he was chosen to spread Kriya Yoga to the West. His address on The Science of Religion was well received, and after a transcontinental speaking tour he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> in 1925. By 1952 the Self-Realization Fellowship had over 100 centres in India and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and he initiated about 100,000 students into Kriya Yoga.</p><p>He taught the unity of the original teachings of Jesus Christ and the original yoga taught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. In 1935 he incorporated the Fellowship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, and from 1935 to 1936 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was met at Howrah Station by a large crowd. He became a naturalised US citizen in 1949. Disputes marked his career: in 1929 his friend Basu Kumar Bagchi sued him, and DNA tests in 2002 ended a paternity claim by the family of a disciple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 7 March 1952 Yogananda spoke at a dinner at the Biltmore Hotel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> for the Indian Ambassador, Binay Ranjan Sen. According to Daya Mata, who was present, he finished by reading his poem My India and then slumped to the floor. He had chosen Rajarsi Janakananda to succeed him as president of the Fellowship.</p><p>Autobiography of a Yogi, published in 1946, has been translated into 45 languages and has sold more than four million copies, and a panel convened by HarperCollins named it one of the 100 most important spiritual books of the 20th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> issued commemorative stamps in 1977 and 2017. The Los Angeles Times called him the twentieth century's first superstar guru.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paramahansa Yogananda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
